---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — The Understory"
  description="Why The Understory exists: for the self-taught developer who can build things but freezes when asked why."
>
  <div class="container-narrow about">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">About</span>
    </nav>

    <p class="section-label">The why</p>
    <h1>I kept meeting developers who could build anything — and explain nothing.</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        They could ship a landing page, wire up a form, follow any tutorial.
        But ask them <em>why</em> the browser repaints, or what the DOM
        actually is, or what happens between pressing Enter and seeing a
        page — and the room went quiet. Not because they were slow. Because
        nobody ever taught them the <strong>layer beneath the code</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's the gap The Understory exists to close. CS grads absorb this
        layer by osmosis — the vocabulary, the history, the mental models.
        Self-taught developers get YouTube tutorials that teach the loop and
        skip the ground it stands on. This is the missing ground: 193 daily
        readings across 18 modules, from "what is a computer, really" to
        shipping and maintaining real software.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          Most tutorials teach you how to write a for-loop. The Understory
          teaches you what actually happens when you press Enter.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <h2>Not a course. A reading room.</h2>
      <p>
        There are no quizzes here, no streaks, no leaderboards. One short
        read a day, and a simple ritual borrowed from Richard Feynman:
        <strong>read, explain it out loud, write a three-sentence summary</strong>.
        If you can't explain it simply, the lesson isn't done — and the site
        won't pretend it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything is a plain Markdown file in a public vault. No accounts,
        no tracking, no ads. Your progress lives in your browser and nowhere
        else. The whole thing builds to static HTML and ships from a hedge
        of scripts to any corner of the world that asks.
      </p>
      <h2>Start anywhere. Or start at Day 1.</h2>
      <p>
        The curriculum is a path, but the rooms open in every direction:
        browse the <a href="/modules">module map</a>, wander the
        <a href="/glossary">glossary</a>, or search for the thing you've
        always pretended to understand. It's yours.
      </p>
      <p class="signoff">
        Built slowly, in public.<br />— The Understory
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .crumbs {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-l);
  }

  .crumbs a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .crumbs a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .about h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .prose {
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
  }

  .signoff {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>

